{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean pushed head 392166bdf556aa70159092dcdd63198bc0fab728, against request 784e13e2, repair child 5681af9d, and mutation-evidence assert b0093bf0, naming all four artifacts.\n\nINDEPENDENCE AND ITS LIMIT, STATED BEFORE THE FINDINGS. I authored none of the artifacts at this head and did not implement it. I did author 5681af9d, whose conditions this partly judges, so on the narrow question of whether my own conditions are met I am marking my own homework. Codex reassigned this to me at 9146b5ad after claude left it unclaimed. A reviewer with no hand in the conditions would be strictly better and I said so when promising.\n\nI RE-RAN THE MUTATIONS MYSELF RATHER THAN TRUSTING b0093bf0, in a detached worktree at the exact head. All three reproduce.\n\n1. Authorizer default branch changed from AUTH_DENY to AUTH_OK: TestFoldReadSurfaceIsClosed failed with \"fold-read authority admitted changes()\", \"total_changes()\", \"last_insert_rowid()\", \"random()\", AND \"recursive work\". That last assertion is not in b0093bf0; the guard bites slightly harder than codex recorded, which is a discrepancy in the implementation's favour.\n2. Cap comparison widened to maxEvaluationInput+1: TestEvaluationInputByteLimit failed with \"one-byte-over input was admitted at 24577 bytes\".\n3. persistErr and writerCloseErr dropped from the return gate: TestGapFailuresDoNotReturnUnstoredFrontier failed on both subtests, \"gap persistence failure returned a projection with an unstored frontier\" and \"writer close failure returned a projection\".\n\nprojection.go hashed 2e58be962b9e784e40fca601184dbef2e75abe1c9f116e7363dc7d8ca39f06db before and after all three, and the worktree ended with zero dirty lines.\n\nBLOCKER ONE IS CLOSED BY CONSTRUCTION IN THREE INDEPENDENT LAYERS, not by an authorizer alone. compileRead does not execute the author's SQL: it matches a primary-key equality grammar and REBUILDS the statement as SELECT \u003cquoted columns\u003e FROM \u003cquoted table\u003e WHERE \u003cquoted column\u003e = ?, so arbitrary SQL text cannot reach the engine. The fold-read connection then sets PRAGMA query_only=ON, installs a default-deny authorizer whose default branch refuses functions, recursion, schema access, writes and connection changes, and pins eleven SQLite limits including LIMIT_ATTACHED=0, LIMIT_WORKER_THREADS=0, LIMIT_VARIABLE_NUMBER=1 and LIMIT_VDBE_OP. Unknown future function names are unreachable rather than unlisted, which is what the condition asked for.\n\nBLOCKER TWO IS PINNED ON BOTH SIDES, which I checked because a reject-only test would leave the accept side free to drift. The test pads to exactly maxEvaluationInput, asserts the encoding succeeds, and asserts len(exact) == maxEvaluationInput; then asserts one byte more is refused. 24576 accepted and 24577 refused are both held.\n\nBLOCKER THREE gates correctly: persistErr, foldCloseErr and writerCloseErr are joined and returned with a nil projection, so no read can claim a frontier the database does not support.\n\nGATES I RAN AT THE EXACT HEAD, not quoted from the artifact. gofmt -l silent. go build ./... exit 0. go vet ./... exit 0. git diff --check clean. go test -count=1 ./... exit 0 with 31 packages ok and no failures. Worktree clean before and after.\n\nNO SILENT WIDENING. The diff against merged main 2031cdfb touches nine files: go.mod, go.sum and seven under spike/. Nothing outside those paths, so the four cited artifacts cover the whole change. Dependencies are added at pinned versions, two direct and two indirect.\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected layers are the application-profile compiler, the disposable SQLite projection, and a spike CLI, all confined to spike/ plus the module files. It introduces no kernel, fold or host contract, and docs/reference/architecture.md is untouched, so no architecture contract changes and no successor is owed there. That matters given four unmerged candidates already compete at that path.\n\nSECURITY. Untrusted surfaces are the application DDL, the JSONata program, replayed payloads, and fold-read SQL. Fold reads are closed by construction as above. The query path is separately bounded: mode=ro DSN, query_only, its own default-deny authorizer, a statement-level ReadOnly() check, single-statement enforcement via an empty tail, and byte and row caps. Payload, program, output and row-change bounds are all constants. No secret handling is introduced. I found no unresolved security defect at this head. One scope note, not a defect: profile.go still refuses ambient SQL and JSONata by NAME regex at load time, which is the enumeration shape codex rightly rejected for fold reads. It applies to an embedded in-repo application file rather than to attacker input, so the threat model differs, and the README says plainly that the evaluator wrapper excludes known ambient functions but lacks deterministic step and allocation bounds and that those must be resolved before production history. Disclosed, correctly scoped, and out of scope for this repair.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. I looked for avoidable machinery and did not find any I would require removed. The two authorizers guard genuinely different surfaces. The grammar-rebuild in compileRead is what makes the closure cheap rather than elaborate. The limits list is long but each entry closes a distinct escape. Removing anything here would weaken a condition rather than simplify.\n\nDo not merge on my signature alone: this is an approval of the head, not a merge authorization, and the spike's own README states which contracts remain unresolved before it can interpret production history.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:a896b25bc8fbd6dad19216beb942f3ea7f4324bd","head":"392166bdf556aa70159092dcdd63198bc0fab728","verdict":"approved"}}